Q: Accessing all uncaught exceptions from terminate handler A: <p>There is no standard C++ mechanism to retrieve all uncaught exceptions. You can get the count via <code>std::uncaught_exceptions()</code> and potentially the <em>last</em> exception (the one that violated <code>noexcept</code>) via <code>std::current_exception()</code>, but not the full chain.</p>
<h2><strong>What does work in Terminate Handlers</strong></h2>
<pre><code>#include &lt;exception&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

void my_terminate_handler() {
    std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;Uncaught exceptions count: &quot; 
              &lt;&lt; std::uncaught_exceptions() &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;

    // May retrieve the exception that caused noexcept violation
    if (std::exception_ptr eptr = std::current_exception()) {
        try {
            std::rethrow_exception(eptr);
        } catch (const std::exception&amp; e) {
            std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;Violating exception: &quot; &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
        } catch (...) {
            std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;Unknown exception type\n&quot;;
        }
    }
    std::abort();
}
</code></pre>
<p>The recommended design pattern is instead of trying to access multiple exceptions, prevent the situation:</p>
<pre><code>class SafeDestructor {
public:
    ~SafeDestructor() noexcept {
        try {
            potentially_throwing_cleanup();
        } catch (const std::exception&amp; e) {
            // Log but don't propagate
            log_error(&quot;Destructor cleanup failed: &quot;, e.what());

            // If we're already unwinding, just note it
            if (std::uncaught_exceptions() &gt; 0) {
                log_error(&quot;(During stack unwinding from another exception)&quot;);
            }
        }
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>Or use <code>std::nested_exception</code> to chain exceptions <em>before</em> hitting noexcept:</p>
<pre><code>void throwing_destructor_logic() {
    try {
        might_throw();
    } catch (...) {
        std::throw_with_nested(
            std::runtime_error(&quot;Cleanup failed&quot;));
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>In a nutshell: Ther is no portable way to get <code>std::exception_ptr</code> references to all uncaught exceptions.</p>

Q: How to create siblings and children in SQL when using FOR XML? A: <p>Please try the following solution.</p>
<p><code>FOR XML PATH</code> is a most flexible construct. It allows to compose exactly what is needed.</p>
<p>We are applying it number of times to correspond nesting levels of the desired output XML.</p>
<p><strong>SQL</strong></p>
<pre><code>-- DDL and sample data population, start
DECLARE @tbl TABLE (PARTY_ID INT, PARTY_HAT_COLOR VARCHAR(20), PARTY_TYPE_NAME VARCHAR(50))
INSERT @tbl (PARTY_ID, PARTY_HAT_COLOR, PARTY_TYPE_NAME) VALUES
(101, 'Blue', 'Retirement'),
(102, 'Red', 'Birthday');
-- DDL and sample data population, end

-- just to see
SELECT * FROM @tbl;

-- XML composition
SELECT
    'PARTY_TOWN_USA_LLC'        AS [HEADER_ROW_1],
    'WE_HAVE_FUN_HERE'          AS [HEADER_ROW_2],
    '123_MAIN_ST_ANYTOWN_USA'   AS [HEADER_ROW_3],
    (
        SELECT
            'STATIC_TEXT_ROW_5_HERE' AS [NESTED_HEADER_ROW_5],
            'STATIC_TEXT_ROW_6_HERE' AS [NESTED_HEADER_ROW_6],
            'STATIC_TEXT_ROW_7_HERE' AS [NESTED_HEADER_ROW_7],
            (
                SELECT
                    t.PARTY_ID        AS [PARTY_ID],
                    t.PARTY_HAT_COLOR AS [PARTY_HAT_COLOR],
                    t.PARTY_TYPE_NAME AS [PARTY_TYPE_NAME]
                FROM @tbl AS t
                FOR XML PATH('PARTY_INFO'), TYPE, ROOT('NESTED_HEADER_ROW_5')
            ) -- AS [NESTED_HEADER_ROW_5]
        FOR XML PATH('NESTED_HEADER_ROW_4'), TYPE
    )
FOR XML PATH('PARTY_TOWN_USA'), TYPE;

</code></pre>
<p><strong>Output</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;PARTY_TOWN_USA&gt;
  &lt;HEADER_ROW_1&gt;PARTY_TOWN_USA_LLC&lt;/HEADER_ROW_1&gt;
  &lt;HEADER_ROW_2&gt;WE_HAVE_FUN_HERE&lt;/HEADER_ROW_2&gt;
  &lt;HEADER_ROW_3&gt;123_MAIN_ST_ANYTOWN_USA&lt;/HEADER_ROW_3&gt;
  &lt;NESTED_HEADER_ROW_4&gt;
    &lt;NESTED_HEADER_ROW_5&gt;STATIC_TEXT_ROW_5_HERE&lt;/NESTED_HEADER_ROW_5&gt;
    &lt;NESTED_HEADER_ROW_6&gt;STATIC_TEXT_ROW_6_HERE&lt;/NESTED_HEADER_ROW_6&gt;
    &lt;NESTED_HEADER_ROW_7&gt;STATIC_TEXT_ROW_7_HERE&lt;/NESTED_HEADER_ROW_7&gt;
    &lt;NESTED_HEADER_ROW_5&gt;
      &lt;PARTY_INFO&gt;
        &lt;PARTY_ID&gt;101&lt;/PARTY_ID&gt;
        &lt;PARTY_HAT_COLOR&gt;Blue&lt;/PARTY_HAT_COLOR&gt;
        &lt;PARTY_TYPE_NAME&gt;Retirement&lt;/PARTY_TYPE_NAME&gt;
      &lt;/PARTY_INFO&gt;
      &lt;PARTY_INFO&gt;
        &lt;PARTY_ID&gt;102&lt;/PARTY_ID&gt;
        &lt;PARTY_HAT_COLOR&gt;Red&lt;/PARTY_HAT_COLOR&gt;
        &lt;PARTY_TYPE_NAME&gt;Birthday&lt;/PARTY_TYPE_NAME&gt;
      &lt;/PARTY_INFO&gt;
    &lt;/NESTED_HEADER_ROW_5&gt;
  &lt;/NESTED_HEADER_ROW_4&gt;
&lt;/PARTY_TOWN_USA&gt;

</code></pre>
